window.ESSAY_BANK6 = [

/* ================== CHAPTER 1 ================== */
{ id:"M1-01", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain why investment banking income is less stable than commercial banking income.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Commercial banking income is dominated by the <b>net interest margin</b> on a loan book that turns over slowly. Loans are contracted for years, so interest income accrues steadily and is largely insulated from short-term market sentiment.</p><p>Investment banking income is <b>transaction-based</b>: advisory fees are earned only when a deal completes, underwriting spreads only when an issue is placed, and trading revenue depends on market volumes and price movements. All of these depend on <b>confidence</b>. In a downturn IPOs are pulled, M&amp;A is postponed and issuance windows close, so several revenue lines fall together — they are positively correlated with the same underlying driver rather than diversifying one another.</p><p>The instability is amplified by the <b>cost base</b>. Compensation is the largest expense and is sticky in the short run, because the franchise depends on retaining people. Revenue therefore falls faster than costs, and profit swings far more than revenue — high operating leverage in a business whose revenue is inherently cyclical.</p>",
  rubric:"Contrast of interest margin with transaction income (0.8) · correlation of revenue lines with confidence (0.6) · fixed compensation base and operating leverage (0.6)." },

{ id:"M1-02", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the trustee and servicer functions of an investment bank.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>trustee</b> function involves acting on the client's behalf under a trust or mandate: executing transactions the client has decided upon, and managing investments where discretion has been delegated. It is an <b>agency</b> function — the bank holds or manages assets for the benefit of another, owes fiduciary-type duties of loyalty and care, and is paid a fee. In debt markets a trustee also acts for bondholders collectively, monitoring covenants and enforcing the indenture on their behalf.</p><p>The <b>servicer</b> function is the provision of <b>prime brokerage</b> to hedge funds and other professional investors. It bundles the operational and financing infrastructure a fund needs but does not wish to build: <b>financing</b> of positions through margin lending, <b>securities lending</b> to support short selling, <b>clearing and settlement</b>, <b>custody</b> of assets, consolidated <b>reporting</b>, and capital introduction to potential investors.</p><p>Both are agency functions earning fees rather than spreads, but prime brokerage does expose the bank to <b>credit risk</b> on the financing it extends — as the 2021 Archegos losses demonstrated, a prime broker's exposure to a single leveraged client can be substantial.</p>",
  rubric:"Trustee role incl. execution and discretionary management (0.7) · at least four prime-brokerage services (0.8) · agency classification and the credit-risk caveat (0.5)." },

{ id:"M1-03", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what is meant by 'the investment bank rents out its balance sheet' and give two examples.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The phrase captures the fact that clients frequently need not advice but the temporary use of someone else's <b>capital and risk-bearing capacity</b>. The bank commits its own balance sheet for a period, absorbs a risk the client cannot or will not carry, and charges for doing so.</p><p><b>Example 1 — firm-commitment underwriting.</b> The issuer needs certainty that it will receive a known sum on a known date. The bank purchases the entire issue at an agreed price and resells it, holding whatever it cannot place. It has lent its balance sheet to absorb <b>placement risk</b>, and the gross spread is the rent.</p><p><b>Example 2 — market making.</b> An investor wishing to sell immediately needs a counterparty now, not when a natural buyer appears. The dealer buys the stock into its own inventory and carries it until it can be offloaded, absorbing <b>inventory and adverse-selection risk</b>. The bid-ask spread is the rent.</p><p>(<i>Bridge financing</i> in M&amp;A and <i>margin lending</i> are equally valid examples.) In each case the economic service is the same: converting the client's uncertainty into certainty by taking the risk onto the bank's own book — which is why these activities consume regulatory capital and why post-crisis capital rules reduced the industry's willingness to supply them.</p>",
  rubric:"Correct interpretation of the phrase (0.6) · two valid examples with the specific risk absorbed (0.9) · link to capital consumption or regulation (0.5)." },

{ id:"M1-04", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Describe the responsibilities of the middle office and explain why it must be independent of the front office.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>middle office</b> measures and controls the risk the front office takes. Its principal functions are <b>market and credit risk management</b> (setting and monitoring limits, computing VaR, running stress tests); <b>compliance</b> (surveillance, conflicts management, regulatory reporting, maintaining restricted and watch lists); <b>treasury</b> (funding and liquidity management); and <b>product control</b> (independent price verification and daily P&amp;L attribution).</p><p><b>Why independence is essential.</b> Front-office staff are compensated on the revenue they report, so they have a direct financial interest in the valuations and risk figures the middle office produces. If a trader could mark their own positions, set their own limits, or influence those who do, then losses could be concealed and limits breached without consequence — the pattern common to every major rogue-trading loss.</p><p>Independence therefore requires separate <b>reporting lines</b> (to the chief risk officer and ultimately the board, not to the desk head), separate <b>compensation</b> not linked to desk revenue, and the <b>authority</b> to escalate and to halt activity. Segregation of duties — traders cannot value, confirm or settle their own trades — is the specific control this independence exists to guarantee.</p>",
  rubric:"At least three middle-office functions (0.7) · conflict arising from revenue-linked pay (0.6) · independent reporting lines, compensation and authority, plus segregation of duties (0.7)." },

{ id:"M1-05", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the role of credit rating agencies in the operating environment of an investment bank.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Role.</b> Rating agencies assess an issuer's or an instrument's creditworthiness and publish an opinion in a standardised symbol. In a debt issue the rating is the principal determinant of the <b>credit spread</b>, and therefore of the coupon the issuer pays. It also determines the <b>investor base</b>, because many institutional mandates permit only investment-grade holdings, and it feeds into regulatory capital calculations for holders.</p><p><b>How the bank interacts with them.</b> <b>Rating advisory</b> is a core DCM service: preparing the issuer's presentation, modelling the effect of the proposed structure on key ratios, and anticipating the agencies' questions. The bank also structures instruments to achieve a target rating.</p><p><b>The recognised weakness</b> is the <b>issuer-pays</b> model: the entity being rated pays for the rating, creating an incentive to compete for business by being accommodating. This conflict contributed materially to the 2008 crisis, when structured products carried ratings that proved unjustified. Post-crisis reforms addressed disclosure of methodologies, conflict management and reduced regulatory reliance on ratings — but the essential conflict remains, which is why an analyst should treat a rating as one input rather than as a substitute for independent credit analysis.</p>",
  rubric:"Rating's effect on spread and investor base (0.7) · rating advisory as a bank service (0.5) · issuer-pays conflict with the 2008 illustration and the analytical implication (0.8)." },

{ id:"M1-06", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what 'material non-public information' means and the obligations it creates.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Information is <b>material</b> if a reasonable investor would consider it important in making an investment decision — that is, if its disclosure would be likely to affect the security's price. It is <b>non-public</b> until it has been disseminated to the market generally and the market has had an opportunity to absorb it. Typical examples are an unannounced acquisition, an unpublished earnings result, an impending regulatory decision, or the loss of a major contract.</p><p><b>Obligations.</b> A person in possession of MNPI must <b>not trade</b> in the affected securities, must <b>not cause or encourage others to trade</b> (tipping), and must <b>not disclose</b> the information outside the barrier without proper authorisation. Where the information arrives inadvertently, the correct response is to notify <b>compliance immediately</b> so the security can be placed on the restricted list. Institutionally the obligations are discharged through the <b>information barrier</b> between private and public sides, documented <b>wall-crossing</b> procedures, restricted and watch lists, personal-account dealing rules and trade surveillance.</p><p>Breach is not merely an ethical failure: insider dealing is a <b>criminal or administrative offence</b> in most jurisdictions, including Vietnam, and exposes both the individual and the firm to sanction and severe reputational damage.</p>",
  rubric:"Both limbs of the definition with examples (0.7) · three obligations — not trade, not tip, not disclose — plus escalation (0.7) · institutional controls and the legal consequence (0.6)." },

{ id:"M1-07", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain how the investment banking industry differs between the United States, continental Europe and Vietnam.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>United States.</b> Historically characterised by <b>legal separation</b>: Glass-Steagall (1933) forced firms to choose between commercial and investment banking, creating the standalone Wall Street investment bank. Gramm-Leach-Bliley (1999) repealed the separation, and the 2008 crisis then eliminated the independent model when Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley became bank holding companies. Supervision sits with the SEC and, since 2008, also the Federal Reserve.</p><p><b>Continental Europe.</b> Predominantly the <b>universal banking</b> model, in which a single institution has long provided both commercial and investment banking services, with no equivalent of the Glass-Steagall separation. Supervision centres on the <b>central bank</b>. Britain is the exception within Europe, having developed specialised merchant and investment banks separately from clearing banks.</p><p><b>Vietnam.</b> The functions are performed not by entities called investment banks but by <b>securities companies</b> (SSI, HSC and others), <b>fund management companies</b> (VFM and others) and <b>partially universal banks</b> — commercial banks conducting securities activity through separately licensed subsidiaries. Securities investment companies are provided for in law but are not established in practice. Supervision is by the <b>State Securities Commission</b> under the Law on Securities, with each operation — brokerage, proprietary trading, underwriting, advisory — separately licensed and subject to its own minimum charter capital.</p>",
  rubric:"US separation-repeal-conversion sequence with supervisor (0.7) · European universal model with the UK exception (0.5) · Vietnamese entities, SSC supervision and per-operation licensing (0.8)." },

{ id:"M1-08", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain why an investment bank's advisory business is described as reputation-dependent, and how reputation is built and lost.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Why it depends on reputation.</b> Advisory services cannot be inspected before purchase. A client choosing an adviser for a once-in-a-generation transaction cannot test the quality of judgement in advance, and the consequences of poor advice are severe and irreversible. The client must also hand over its most sensitive information. In these conditions the client selects on <b>credibility</b> — evidence of past execution, sector expertise, discretion and independence — because that is the only signal available.</p><p><b>How reputation is built.</b> Slowly, through completed transactions that stand up over time; through league-table position as visible evidence of mandate flow; through published research and sector expertise; through senior bankers' individual track records and relationships; and through demonstrated discretion — clients notice when a bank does <i>not</i> leak.</p><p><b>How it is lost.</b> Quickly and often through a single episode: a leak of confidential information; a conflict handled badly, such as research shaped to win a mandate; a mis-sold product; a regulatory finding or public enforcement action; or a high-profile transaction that destroys client value. Because the asset is intangible and the loss is abrupt, a bank may rationally <b>decline a profitable mandate</b> whose reputational risk outweighs the fee — the fee is earned once, whereas reputation determines the flow of all future mandates.</p>",
  rubric:"Credence-good argument incl. inability to inspect quality and sensitivity of information (0.8) · at least three ways reputation is built (0.5) · at least three ways it is lost, with the asymmetry and the declining-a-mandate implication (0.7)." },

{ id:"M1-09", ch:1, type:"case", clo:"CLO1", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A bank is asked to advise a state-owned enterprise on its equitization while another team in the same bank is advising a private investor interested in buying a stake. Analyse the problem and the options.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — the problem.</b> This is a direct <b>conflict of interest</b>: the bank would be advising the <b>seller</b> (the SOE and, behind it, the State) on maximising value while advising a <b>prospective buyer</b> on acquiring the same asset at the lowest price. The two mandates are in irreconcilable opposition on the central issue — price.</p><p>The problem is aggravated by three features. First, the <b>information asymmetry</b>: the sell-side team will hold detailed non-public information about the enterprise's valuation, its floor price and the State's flexibility, which would be of extraordinary value to the buy-side team. Second, the <b>public interest</b>: equitization involves the disposal of state assets, where undervaluation transfers public wealth to private buyers and attracts intense scrutiny and potential criminal liability. Third, <b>appearance</b>: even if the barrier holds perfectly, the perception that a single adviser stood on both sides may taint the transaction and expose all parties to challenge.</p><p><b>Options.</b> (1) <b>Decline one mandate.</b> This is the cleanest response and, given the public-interest dimension, the appropriate default — normally declining the buy-side role, since the sell-side mandate carries the public duty. (2) <b>Accept only with full informed consent from both clients</b>, supported by a rigorous information barrier: physically and systemically separated teams, separate document storage, no shared personnel including at senior and support level, and independent supervision of each team. Even then, consent from the State-side client may not be obtainable or appropriate. (3) <b>Chinese-walled teams with an independent adviser</b> appointed for one side. (4) In practice, transparent <b>competitive bidding</b> is the strongest protection for the seller regardless of adviser arrangements, since a genuine auction reveals value that no valuation opinion can guarantee.</p><p><b>Recommendation.</b> Decline the buy-side mandate. The reputational and legal exposure in a state-asset disposal is disproportionate to the fee, and the conflict goes to the central term of the transaction rather than to a peripheral matter.</p>",
  rubric:"Conflict correctly identified as opposed interests on price (0.8) · information asymmetry, public-interest and appearance aggravators (0.9) · at least three options incl. declining and barriers with informed consent (0.9) · reasoned recommendation (0.4)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 2 ================== */
{ id:"M2-01", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain the purpose of the prospectus and the liability attaching to it.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Purpose.</b> The prospectus is the formal disclosure document by which an issuer offers securities to the public. Its function is to place investors in a position to make an <b>informed investment decision</b>, by disclosing all information material to that decision. It also creates a single authoritative source of information, so that all investors receive the same disclosure at the same time — which is why promotional statements outside it are restricted during the <b>quiet period</b>.</p><p><b>Contents</b> typically include a description of the business and its markets; audited financial statements and management's discussion of them; the offering terms and the intended <b>use of proceeds</b>; <b>risk factors</b>; details of management, governance and major shareholders; related-party transactions; and material contracts and litigation.</p><p><b>Liability.</b> The document carries statutory liability for material misstatement or omission. Responsibility attaches to the <b>issuer</b> and its <b>directors</b>, and in most regimes also to the <b>underwriters</b> — which is precisely why underwriters conduct their own due diligence and obtain comfort letters from auditors and legal opinions. Their exposure is the mechanism that makes the disclosure credible: the bank is staking its own liability on the accuracy of what the issuer says.</p>",
  rubric:"Informed-decision purpose and equal disclosure (0.6) · at least four content items incl. risk factors and use of proceeds (0.6) · liability of issuer, directors and underwriters, linked to due diligence (0.8)." },

{ id:"M2-02", ch:2, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"A company with 80 million shares trading at VND 55,000 makes a 1-for-5 rights issue at VND 35,000. Compute the funds raised, the TERP, the value of a right, and the total market capitalisation before and after.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> New shares = 80m ÷ 5 = <b>16 million</b>.<br>Funds raised = 16m × 35,000 = <b>VND 560 billion</b>.</p><p>TERP = (5 × 55,000 + 1 × 35,000) ÷ 6 = (275,000 + 35,000) ÷ 6 = 310,000 ÷ 6 = <b>VND 51,667</b>.</p><p>Value of one right = 55,000 − 51,667 = <b>VND 3,333</b>.</p><p><b>Market capitalisation:</b><br>Before = 80m × 55,000 = <b>VND 4,400 billion</b><br>After = 96m × 51,667 = <b>VND 4,960 billion</b><br>Increase = VND 560 billion — <b>exactly the cash raised</b>.</p><p>This confirms the internal consistency of the calculation: a rights issue neither creates nor destroys value at the moment of issue. The company's market capitalisation rises by precisely the new cash injected, and the share price falls to the TERP only because the same total value is now spread over more shares. Whether value is subsequently created depends entirely on what the company does with the VND 560 billion.</p>",
  rubric:"New shares and funds raised (0.5) · TERP (0.5) · value of right (0.4) · both market caps with the reconciliation to funds raised (0.6)." },

{ id:"M2-03", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the advantages and disadvantages of becoming a public company.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — advantages.</b> <b>Access to capital</b> on better terms and in larger amounts, both at IPO and through subsequent issuance. <b>Liquidity</b> and an observable market price for existing shareholders, enabling partial monetisation and providing a valuation for estate and succession purposes. <b>Currency for acquisitions</b> — listed shares can be offered as consideration — and for <b>employee incentives</b> through share schemes. <b>Reputation and credibility</b> with banks, customers, suppliers and regulators. <b>Governance discipline</b>, which although a cost is also a benefit in professionalising the company.</p><p><b>Disadvantages.</b> The <b>cost</b> of the offering itself — underwriting spread, legal, audit and listing fees — and of continuing compliance. <b>Continuous disclosure</b> obligations, including of information a private company would prefer to keep from competitors. <b>Short-term pressure</b> from the market and analysts, which may distort decision-making away from long-term investment. <b>Dilution</b> and loss of absolute control, with exposure to activist investors and to <b>takeover</b>. Greater <b>personal responsibility and scrutiny</b> for directors. And the burden falls disproportionately on smaller companies, for which fixed compliance costs are proportionately larger.</p><p>The decision therefore turns on the amount of capital genuinely needed, the owners' tolerance for disclosure and dilution, and whether the company is operationally and organisationally ready.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four advantages (0.7) · at least four disadvantages (0.8) · balanced concluding judgement on the decision criteria (0.5)." },

{ id:"M2-04", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what venture capital is and why it requires a portfolio approach.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Venture capital</b> is the financing of early-stage, high-growth companies in <b>staged rounds</b> — seed, Series A, B, C — in exchange for a <b>minority</b> equity stake with protective and control rights, and without leverage. The investor supplies not only capital but governance, networks, recruitment and strategic support, and expects to exit within roughly five to ten years through an IPO or trade sale.</p><p><b>Why a portfolio approach is essential.</b> The return distribution is extremely <b>skewed</b>. Most early-stage companies fail entirely or return little; a minority return the capital invested; and a very small number produce outcomes many times the investment. Because the failures are frequent and the winners rare, no individual investment can be relied upon — the expected return of any single company is dominated by a low-probability, high-magnitude outcome.</p><p>A fund must therefore hold enough investments that the probability of capturing at least one outsized winner becomes high, which is why VC funds typically hold twenty to forty positions. It also explains two characteristic practices: <b>staged financing</b>, which allows the investor to abandon failing companies cheaply and concentrate follow-on capital on those succeeding, and the acceptance of a high failure rate as a <b>designed feature</b> of the model rather than evidence of poor selection. Judging a VC fund by the proportion of investments that fail is therefore a category error; it should be judged on the fund-level multiple and IRR.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition incl. staged rounds, minority stake, no leverage (0.6) · skewed return distribution requiring diversification (0.7) · staged financing and failure rate as design, with the evaluation implication (0.7)." },

{ id:"M2-05", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what determines whether an issuer chooses equity or debt to raise capital.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The choice turns on several considerations.</p><p><b>Cost and tax.</b> Debt is cheaper because it is senior and less risky to the investor, and its interest is <b>tax-deductible</b>, whereas dividends are not. This favours debt where the issuer has taxable profits to shelter.</p><p><b>Financial risk and capacity.</b> Debt creates <b>fixed obligations</b>. A company with volatile cash flows, high operating leverage or heavy capital expenditure has limited capacity to bear them; a stable, cash-generative business has more. Existing leverage, covenant headroom and rating targets constrain how much further debt is available.</p><p><b>Dilution and control.</b> Equity dilutes existing shareholders' ownership, earnings per share and voting control — a decisive consideration for a family or founder-controlled company. Debt does not.</p><p><b>Signalling.</b> Under <b>pecking-order</b> reasoning, issuing equity signals that management may consider the shares overvalued, so equity issues often meet a negative share-price reaction; debt issuance carries no such signal. Firms therefore prefer internal funds first, then debt, and issue equity last.</p><p><b>Purpose and market conditions.</b> Long-lived, tangible assets suit debt with matched maturity; uncertain, intangible or growth investment suits equity, which requires no fixed servicing. Finally, the practical question of which market is <b>open</b> and on what terms often determines timing.</p>",
  rubric:"Tax shield and relative cost (0.5) · cash-flow capacity and existing leverage (0.5) · dilution and control (0.4) · pecking-order signalling (0.3) · asset-matching or market conditions (0.3)." },

{ id:"M2-06", ch:2, type:"case", clo:"CLO1", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"An underwriter's book is only 70% covered two days before pricing. Analyse the options and their consequences for each party.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — the problem.</b> An undersubscribed book means demand at the marketed price range is insufficient. Proceeding unchanged would leave the underwriter holding unsold stock (under a firm commitment) and would almost certainly produce a weak aftermarket, damaging the issuer's reputation and every investor allocated.</p><p><b>Options and consequences.</b></p><p>(1) <b>Reduce the price</b>, pricing below or at the bottom of the range. <i>Issuer:</i> raises less capital and accepts a lower valuation, which also sets a low reference for future issuance. <i>Investors:</i> better entry price, so demand should firm. <i>Underwriter:</i> smaller fee but materially reduced placement risk. This is the most common response.</p><p>(2) <b>Reduce the size</b> of the offering, selling fewer shares at the intended price. <i>Issuer:</i> preserves the valuation but raises less than planned, which may not fund its stated use of proceeds — a problem if the prospectus specified an investment programme. <i>Underwriter:</i> risk reduced; the smaller free float may impair aftermarket liquidity.</p><p>(3) <b>Combine</b> a modest price reduction with a modest size reduction — often the practical compromise.</p><p>(4) <b>Postpone</b> the offering. <i>Issuer:</i> avoids a bad price but incurs wasted cost, signals weakness to the market, and must refresh its financial disclosure before returning. <i>Underwriter:</i> avoids loss but earns nothing after substantial work.</p><p>(5) <b>Proceed unchanged.</b> Rarely defensible: the underwriter absorbs the shortfall and then sells into the market, depressing the price and harming allocated investors — reputationally damaging to both bank and issuer.</p><p><b>Judgement.</b> The correct course depends on whether the shortfall reflects <b>price</b> or <b>market conditions</b>. If investors like the company but not the valuation, reprice. If the whole market is closed, postpone. The underwriter's duty is to give the issuer an honest reading of the book, even though a completed deal earns a fee and a postponed one does not — a genuine conflict, since the bank is paid on completion.</p>",
  rubric:"Problem correctly framed (0.4) · at least four options (1.0) · consequences traced for issuer, investors and underwriter (1.0) · reasoned judgement incl. the completion-fee conflict (0.6)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 3 ================== */
{ id:"M3-01", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain the difference between a strategic buyer and a financial buyer in an M&A auction.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>strategic buyer</b> is an operating company, usually in the same or an adjacent industry. Its motive is <b>industrial</b>: it acquires to realise <b>synergies</b> — removing duplicated costs, gaining market access, securing supply or acquiring technology. It generally intends to hold indefinitely and to integrate the target into its own operations.</p><p>A <b>financial buyer</b> is a private-equity sponsor. Its motive is <b>financial return</b> over a defined horizon of roughly three to seven years. It has no operations to combine with the target, so it cannot capture synergies; its returns come from <b>leverage and deleveraging</b>, operational improvement, and exit multiple.</p><p><b>Consequences for the auction.</b> Strategic buyers can usually <b>pay more</b>, because synergies are value only they can realise, and they are less constrained by financing markets. Financial buyers are disciplined by the arithmetic of their required IRR and by what lenders will advance, so their price is effectively capped by the debt market.</p><p>But strategic buyers bring drawbacks for the seller: they are competitors, so sharing sensitive information in diligence is dangerous if the deal fails; they may face <b>competition clearance</b> risk and longer timetables; and integration may mean job losses, which matters where management or the State is the seller. Financial buyers typically offer <b>speed, certainty and confidentiality</b>, and often retain management. A well-run process therefore runs both types in parallel, managing information carefully and using the tension between them.</p>",
  rubric:"Both types correctly characterised by motive and horizon (0.8) · synergy capacity explaining higher strategic pricing and the debt-market cap on sponsors (0.6) · at least two offsetting considerations (0.6)." },

{ id:"M3-02", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain what a teaser and a Confidential Information Memorandum are, and why they are separate documents.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>teaser</b> is a short document, usually one or two pages, circulated to potential buyers at the start of a sell-side process. It is <b>anonymous</b> — it describes the business generically ('a leading Vietnamese consumer goods manufacturer with revenue of approximately VND 1,500 billion') without naming it. Its purpose is to generate interest and identify which parties are worth engaging.</p><p>The <b>Confidential Information Memorandum (CIM)</b> is the substantive marketing document, typically fifty to a hundred pages, released only after a recipient has signed a <b>non-disclosure agreement</b>. It identifies the company and describes its history, products, markets, competitive position, management, operations and detailed historical and projected financials.</p><p><b>Why they are separate.</b> The separation implements a <b>staged disclosure</b> principle. At the outset the seller does not know who is genuinely interested, and revealing that the business is for sale is itself damaging: employees may leave, customers and suppliers may reconsider, and competitors gain intelligence. The anonymous teaser tests interest at minimal cost. Only parties who declare interest and accept binding confidentiality obligations receive identifying and commercially sensitive information. Information is then released progressively — CIM, then management presentations, then the data room — with the most sensitive material withheld until late, particularly from competitors.</p>",
  rubric:"Teaser described incl. anonymity and purpose (0.6) · CIM described incl. NDA gate and contents (0.6) · staged-disclosure rationale with the specific harms of premature disclosure (0.8)." },

{ id:"M3-03", ch:3, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:3, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Acquirer P has EPS VND 4,500, 120m shares, price VND 72,000. Target Q has net income VND 216bn, 90m shares, unaffected price VND 18,000. P offers a 33% premium, 70% in shares and 30% in cash at 5.5% after-tax cost. Synergies after tax are VND 45bn. Compute pro forma EPS and accretion, and state the breakeven synergy level.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Offer price = 18,000 × 1.33 = <b>VND 23,940</b>.<br>Equity purchase price = 23,940 × 90m = <b>VND 2,154.6bn</b>.</p><p>Share consideration = 70% × 2,154.6 = VND 1,508.22bn → new shares = 1,508.22bn ÷ 72,000 = <b>20.947m</b>.<br>Cash consideration = 30% × 2,154.6 = VND 646.38bn → after-tax interest = 5.5% × 646.38 = <b>VND 35.55bn</b>.</p><p>P's net income = 4,500 × 120m = <b>VND 540bn</b>.<br>Pro forma net income = 540 + 216 + 45 − 35.55 = <b>VND 765.45bn</b>.<br>Pro forma shares = 120 + 20.947 = <b>140.947m</b>.<br>Pro forma EPS = 765.45bn ÷ 140.947m = <b>VND 5,431</b>.<br>Accretion = 5,431 ÷ 4,500 − 1 = <b>+20.7% accretive</b>.</p><p><b>Breakeven synergies.</b> For EPS to be unchanged at VND 4,500, pro forma net income must equal 4,500 × 140.947m = <b>VND 634.26bn</b>.<br>Required synergies = 634.26 − 540 − 216 + 35.55 = <b>−VND 86.19bn</b>.</p><p>The breakeven is <b>negative</b>, meaning the transaction is accretive even with <b>zero</b> synergies — indeed it would tolerate VND 86bn of negative synergies before diluting. The reason is visible in the multiples: P trades at 72,000 ÷ 4,500 = <b>16.0×</b> earnings while paying 2,154.6 ÷ 216 = <b>10.0×</b> for Q. Issuing shares valued at 16× to buy earnings costing 10× is accretive on arithmetic alone. The caution remains that accretion is not value creation: whether the 33% premium is justified depends on the present value of the synergies, not on the EPS outcome.</p>",
  rubric:"Offer price and purchase value (0.5) · new shares and interest (0.6) · pro forma NI, shares, EPS (0.8) · accretion (0.3) · breakeven synergy computed and correctly interpreted as negative (0.5) · P/E explanation and accretion≠value caveat (0.3)." },

{ id:"M3-04", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the role of the investment bank on each side of an M&A transaction.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — sell-side.</b> The bank's objective is to maximise price and certainty for the seller. It prepares the business for sale and advises on readiness and timing; performs the <b>valuation</b> and sets expectations; designs the <b>process</b>, choosing between a broad auction and a targeted approach; prepares the teaser and CIM; identifies and approaches the buyer universe; manages the data room and due-diligence process; evaluates and compares bids on price, certainty, financing and conditionality; runs the negotiation to preserve <b>competitive tension</b>; and supports signing and closing. It may also provide a <b>fairness opinion</b> to the board.</p><p><b>Buy-side.</b> The bank's objective is to secure the target on the best terms and with acceptable risk. It helps define acquisition criteria and <b>screens targets</b>; makes the approach; values the target standalone and with <b>synergies</b>; coordinates the due-diligence workstreams; advises on <b>structure</b> — share versus asset purchase, consideration mix — and arranges or advises on <b>financing</b>; models <b>accretion/dilution</b> and pro forma leverage; conducts the negotiation; and supports regulatory clearance and integration planning.</p><p><b>Common to both:</b> the bank supplies valuation expertise, process management, market intelligence, negotiating experience and — importantly — an intermediary who can explore positions without the principals losing face. Fees are typically a <b>success fee</b> contingent on completion, sometimes with a retainer, which itself creates an incentive toward completion that the adviser must manage honestly.</p>",
  rubric:"At least five sell-side activities (0.7) · at least five buy-side activities (0.7) · common contributions and the success-fee incentive (0.6)." },

{ id:"M3-05", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the main problems that cause post-merger integration to fail and how they can be mitigated.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — problems.</b> (1) <b>Culture clash</b> — incompatible norms, decision-making styles and expectations, which in people-dependent businesses drives the departure of exactly the staff whose capability was being acquired. (2) <b>Loss of key personnel</b>, often to competitors, taking client relationships with them. (3) <b>Systems and data incompatibility</b>, where migration proves far longer and costlier than assumed, disrupting service. (4) <b>Customer disruption</b> — uncertainty during integration invites competitors to approach the combined entity's customers. (5) <b>Unclear governance</b> — ambiguity about who decides what, producing paralysis. (6) <b>Synergy targets without ownership</b>, announced to the market but assigned to no one accountable. (7) <b>Management distraction</b> from running the underlying businesses.</p><p><b>Mitigation.</b> Begin integration planning <b>before signing</b>, not after closing. Establish <b>Day-1 readiness</b> so that customers, staff and systems experience continuity from the first day. Put <b>retention packages</b> and clear roles in place for identified key staff, communicated early. Define <b>governance and reporting lines</b> immediately, so decision rights are unambiguous. Create a <b>synergy tracker</b> assigning each target a value, a named owner and a deadline, reported regularly to a steering committee. Communicate actively and honestly with customers, suppliers and employees. Address culture explicitly rather than assuming it will resolve itself. And realistically resource the integration, recognising that costs to achieve are substantial and must have been deducted when the premium was justified.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four failure causes (0.8) · at least four mitigations (0.8) · pre-signing planning and the synergy-tracker discipline (0.4)." },

{ id:"M3-06", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what a fairness opinion is, what it does and does not say, and why boards obtain one.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>fairness opinion</b> is a written opinion from a financial adviser stating that the consideration to be paid or received in a transaction is <b>fair, from a financial point of view</b>, to a specified party — usually the target's shareholders. It is supported by the adviser's valuation analysis across the standard methodologies, and it is normally delivered to the board at the meeting at which the transaction is approved.</p><p><b>What it does not say.</b> It does <b>not</b> say the transaction is a good idea, that it is the best available alternative, or that a higher price could not have been obtained. It does not opine on the strategic merits, on the likelihood of realising synergies, on the allocation of consideration among classes of shareholder, or on how any individual shareholder should vote. It addresses only whether the <b>financial terms</b> fall within a range the adviser considers fair, at a point in time and on stated assumptions.</p><p><b>Why boards obtain one.</b> Directors owe fiduciary duties, and in a change-of-control transaction their decision will be scrutinised and may be litigated. A fairness opinion evidences that the board <b>informed itself</b> by obtaining independent professional advice before deciding — supporting the discharge of the duty of care. It is therefore as much a governance and litigation-protection instrument as a valuation one.</p><p>The recognised weakness is that the adviser giving the opinion is frequently also earning a <b>success fee</b> on completion, creating an incentive to find the terms fair. Better practice is to obtain the opinion from an <b>independent</b> adviser not receiving a contingent fee.</p>",
  rubric:"Correct definition incl. 'from a financial point of view' (0.5) · at least three things it does not address (0.6) · fiduciary/litigation rationale (0.5) · contingent-fee conflict and the independent-adviser remedy (0.4)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 4 ================== */
{ id:"M4-01", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what asset allocation is and why it is considered the most important investment decision.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Asset allocation</b> is the division of a portfolio among broad asset classes — equities, bonds, cash, real estate and alternatives. <b>Strategic</b> asset allocation sets the long-run target weights reflecting the investor's objectives and constraints; <b>tactical</b> asset allocation makes shorter-term deviations around those targets within permitted ranges.</p><p><b>Why it dominates.</b> Decades of empirical research find that the strategic allocation explains the great majority of the <b>variability of a portfolio's returns over time</b> — far more than security selection or market timing. The reason is structural: asset classes have distinct and persistent risk-return characteristics and are imperfectly correlated with one another, so the mix determines the portfolio's fundamental risk exposure. Within an asset class, individual securities move largely together with that class, so selection decisions operate on a much smaller residual.</p><p>The practical implications are that the allocation decision should be made <b>first</b> and with the most care; that it must follow directly from the objectives and constraints recorded in the IPS; that it should be reviewed when circumstances change rather than in response to market movements; and that <b>rebalancing</b> is necessary, because market movements cause drift away from the intended risk profile.</p><p>A caveat: the finding concerns variability of returns <i>over time</i> for a given portfolio, and is sometimes overstated as explaining differences in return <i>between</i> managers, where selection matters more.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition with strategic/tactical distinction (0.6) · empirical finding with the correlation-based reason (0.7) · practical implications incl. rebalancing (0.5) · precision about what the finding claims (0.2)." },

{ id:"M4-02", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what an exchange-traded fund is and how its price stays close to net asset value.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> An <b>ETF</b> is an open-end pooled investment vehicle whose units are <b>listed and traded intraday on an exchange</b>, like a share. Most track an index, though active ETFs exist. Investors buy and sell on the exchange from other investors at market prices, rather than subscribing and redeeming with the fund at NAV as in a conventional open-end fund.</p><p><b>How price tracks NAV.</b> The mechanism is arbitrage by <b>authorised participants (APs)</b> — large institutions with the right to transact directly with the fund in large blocks called <b>creation units</b>, usually <b>in kind</b> by delivering or receiving the underlying basket of securities.</p><p>If the ETF's market price rises <b>above</b> NAV, an AP buys the underlying basket, delivers it to the fund in exchange for new ETF units, and sells those units in the market at the higher price, capturing the difference. The selling pressure pushes the price down toward NAV, and the supply of units expands. If the price falls <b>below</b> NAV, the AP buys ETF units cheaply in the market, redeems them with the fund for the underlying basket, and sells the securities — buying pressure lifts the price toward NAV and units are cancelled.</p><p>This arbitrage keeps price and NAV closely aligned, and the in-kind mechanism also makes ETFs tax-efficient and avoids forced selling. Note that it works only while the underlying basket is liquid and priceable — in stressed markets, or for ETFs holding illiquid assets, discounts can open up because the arbitrage becomes difficult or risky to execute.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition incl. intraday exchange trading (0.5) · AP creation/redemption mechanism (0.5) · both arbitrage directions traced correctly (0.7) · limitation in stressed or illiquid markets (0.3)." },

{ id:"M4-03", ch:4, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A portfolio returned 13% with volatility 18% and tracking error 4%; its benchmark returned 10%; the risk-free rate is 5%. Compute the Sharpe ratio and information ratio, and state which is more relevant if this is the client's only investment.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Sharpe ratio = (13 − 5) ÷ 18 = 8 ÷ 18 = <b>0.44</b>.<br>Information ratio = (13 − 10) ÷ 4 = 3 ÷ 4 = <b>0.75</b>.</p><p><b>Which is more relevant.</b> If this portfolio is the client's <b>only</b> investment, the <b>Sharpe ratio</b> is the appropriate criterion. The reason is that the client experiences the <b>total volatility</b> of their wealth — both the systematic risk of being in the market and any specific risk the manager has taken. Sharpe measures excess return over the risk-free rate per unit of that total risk, which is precisely the trade-off the client faces.</p><p>The <b>information ratio</b> answers a different question: how efficiently the manager converts <b>active</b> risk into <b>active</b> return relative to the benchmark. It is the right measure when the portfolio is <b>one mandate within a larger allocation</b> — the investor has already decided to hold that asset class through the benchmark, and is paying the manager only for deviation from it.</p><p>Here the IR of 0.75 is respectable, indicating genuine value added per unit of active risk, while the Sharpe of 0.44 is moderate. The two can rank managers differently: a manager can add value against a benchmark that is itself in an unattractive asset class, which the Sharpe ratio would reveal and the IR would not.</p>",
  rubric:"Both ratios computed correctly (0.8) · Sharpe identified as relevant with the total-wealth reasoning (0.6) · IR's proper context and the possibility of divergent rankings (0.6)." },

{ id:"M4-04", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the classification of asset management services by client segment and how the service model differs.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Institutional mandates</b> serve pension funds, insurers and sovereign funds. Assets are large, the client is professional, and the service is a <b>segregated account</b> managed to a precisely specified mandate and benchmark, with detailed reporting and periodic formal review. Fees are the lowest — often 0.1–0.5% — because of scale and the client's negotiating power.</p><p><b>Retail funds</b> serve the general public through <b>pooled vehicles</b> — mutual funds and ETFs. The product is standardised, since it must serve many investors identically; distribution is through banks, securities companies and advisers; regulation is heaviest because investors are unsophisticated. Fees range widely, from a few basis points for index products to around 2% for active funds.</p><p><b>Private wealth</b> serves high-net-worth individuals through advisory or discretionary accounts with a dedicated relationship manager. The service is <b>tailored</b> and extends beyond investment into tax, retirement, estate and succession planning. Fees are typically 0.5–1.5%. At the ultra-high-net-worth level it extends to bespoke structuring and philanthropy, and at the top to a <b>family office</b> providing a full institutional service for one family.</p><p><b>Alternatives</b> — hedge funds and private equity — serve professional investors under lighter product regulation but with restricted access, illiquidity and <b>performance-based</b> fees of roughly 1–2% plus 20% carry.</p><p>The pattern is that as mandates become more bespoke, less liquid and less constrained, fees rise; as they become more standardised and scalable, fees fall.</p>",
  rubric:"Four segments correctly identified with client type and vehicle (0.9) · service-model differences incl. tailoring and regulation (0.6) · fee levels and the organising principle (0.5)." },

{ id:"M4-05", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what performance attribution analysis is and why it matters to a client.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Performance attribution</b> decomposes a portfolio's return relative to its benchmark into the specific decisions that produced it. The standard decomposition separates:</p><ul><li>the <b>allocation effect</b> — value added or lost by over- or under-weighting sectors, asset classes or countries relative to the benchmark; and</li><li>the <b>selection effect</b> — value added or lost by choosing particular securities within each sector.</li></ul><p>An <b>interaction</b> term captures the combined result of being overweight a sector in which selection was also strong.</p><p><b>Why it matters to the client.</b> It answers whether outperformance was the result of <b>repeatable skill</b> or of a single fortunate bet — a manager whose entire excess return came from one overweight sector has demonstrated something quite different from one adding value consistently across many decisions. It verifies that the manager did what they said: a manager marketed as a stock-picker whose returns come entirely from sector allocation is not following the stated process.</p><p>It also identifies where the manager genuinely adds value, informing whether to retain, redeem or restructure the mandate; and it supports the <b>risk</b> discussion, since attribution shows where active risk was actually taken. Return alone cannot answer any of these questions — which is why attribution, not just performance measurement, forms part of the feedback phase of the portfolio management process.</p>",
  rubric:"Allocation and selection effects correctly defined (0.8) · repeatability/skill-versus-luck argument (0.5) · process verification and retention decision (0.5) · placement in the feedback phase (0.2)." },

{ id:"M4-06", ch:4, type:"case", clo:"CLO2", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A bank's wealth division is set a target to increase sales of in-house funds. Analyse the risks this creates and recommend safeguards.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — risks.</b> (1) <b>Suitability failure.</b> A sales target creates pressure to recommend in-house products irrespective of whether they fit the client's objectives, risk tolerance and capacity — the definition of mis-selling. (2) <b>Conflict of interest.</b> The bank earns more from its own funds than from third-party products, so the recommendation may serve the bank rather than the client, breaching the duty to place client interests first. (3) <b>Concentration risk</b> for clients, whose portfolios become concentrated in one manager's products — and, if the bank is also their deposit-taker and lender, in a single institution. (4) <b>Regulatory and legal exposure</b> — mis-selling attracts sanction, compensation liability and, in Vietnam, has been a live enforcement issue in the distribution of insurance and corporate bonds through banking channels. (5) <b>Reputational damage</b>, which in a trust-based business is the most expensive outcome. (6) <b>Adverse selection of staff behaviour</b> — targets reward volume, so the least scrupulous advisers may outperform on the metric.</p><p><b>Safeguards.</b> Make <b>suitability</b> the binding constraint: no recommendation may proceed without a documented profile, an IPS and a recorded rationale linking the product to the client's stated objectives. Require advisers to present <b>comparable third-party alternatives</b> on an objective basis, and to record why the recommended product was chosen. Provide <b>clear written disclosure</b> of the in-house relationship and any distribution commission. Restructure <b>incentives</b> so that adviser compensation is not driven by product-specific volume — balanced scorecards weighting client outcomes, retention and complaint rates. Implement <b>post-sale monitoring</b>: sample file reviews, concentration reporting, complaint and early-redemption analysis, and mystery shopping. Give <b>compliance</b> authority to block sales and to escalate patterns to the board. Finally, senior management should reconsider the target itself — a volume target on a proprietary product is a structurally unsound incentive, and no amount of downstream control fully neutralises it.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four risks incl. suitability and conflict (1.1) · at least five safeguards incl. suitability documentation, alternatives, disclosure and incentive redesign (1.4) · challenge to the target itself (0.5)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 5 ================== */
{ id:"M5-01", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the PESTEL framework and give one Vietnamese example of each factor affecting the securities industry.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>PESTEL</b> analyses the external macro-environment shaping an industry.</p><ul><li><b>Political</b> — government policy on market development and state divestment; for example the pace of SOE equitization determines the supply of new listings and advisory mandates.</li><li><b>Economic</b> — growth, interest rates, inflation and exchange rates; rising deposit rates draw retail money away from equities, reducing brokerage volumes and margin lending.</li><li><b>Social</b> — demographics and behaviour; a young, digitally literate population with rising incomes has driven rapid growth in retail brokerage accounts.</li><li><b>Technological</b> — online and mobile trading platforms have compressed commissions and shifted competition toward technology and financing services.</li><li><b>Environmental</b> — growing investor demand for ESG disclosure affects which companies attract capital and creates new advisory work.</li><li><b>Legal</b> — the Law on Securities 2019, margin regulations, corporate-bond decrees and listing conditions directly determine what firms may do and on what terms.</li></ul><p>The purpose is not description but <b>discipline</b>: PESTEL identifies which external forces could change the industry's profitability, and those judgements should then be visible in the assumptions of any valuation of a company in it.</p>",
  rubric:"Six factors correctly expanded (0.8) · a plausible Vietnamese example for at least five (0.8) · purpose linked to valuation assumptions (0.4)." },

{ id:"M5-02", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between horizontal and vertical analysis of financial statements and what each reveals.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Horizontal analysis</b> compares each line item <b>across successive periods</b>, expressing the change in absolute or percentage terms — for example revenue rising 12% and then 15% while cost of sales rises 14% and 22%. It reveals <b>trends and growth rates</b>, and, critically, <b>divergences</b>: a line growing materially faster than the line it should track is a warning. Receivables growing far faster than revenue suggests collection problems or aggressive revenue recognition; inventory outpacing cost of sales suggests obsolescence or weakening demand.</p><p><b>Vertical analysis</b> — also called common-size analysis — expresses every item as a <b>percentage of a base figure</b> within a single period: income-statement items as a percentage of revenue, balance-sheet items as a percentage of total assets. It reveals <b>structure and composition</b>: the gross margin, the proportion of revenue consumed by each cost category, the mix of assets, and the balance between debt and equity funding.</p><p><b>Why both are needed.</b> Vertical analysis makes companies of <b>different sizes comparable</b>, both against peers and against industry norms — an essential step before applying comparable-company multiples. Horizontal analysis shows the <b>direction of travel</b> that a single-period snapshot conceals. Used together on several years they identify both the level and the trajectory of performance, and they are the routine first step in the financial due diligence and quality-of-earnings work that precedes any valuation.</p>",
  rubric:"Horizontal analysis defined with the divergence insight (0.7) · vertical analysis defined with what it reveals (0.6) · complementary use incl. cross-company comparability and the link to diligence (0.7)." },

{ id:"M5-03", ch:5, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A company will pay a dividend of VND 4,000 next year. Investors require 13%. (a) Value the share if growth is 5% forever. (b) Value it if growth is 5% for 3 years then 3% forever, with year-4 dividend of VND 4,772. (c) Comment on the difference.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Constant growth: P0 = D1 ÷ (r − g) = 4,000 ÷ (0.13 − 0.05) = 4,000 ÷ 0.08 = <b>VND 50,000</b>.</p><p>(b) Two-stage. Dividends: D1 = 4,000; D2 = 4,200; D3 = 4,410.<br>Discount factors at 13%: 0.8850, 0.7831, 0.6931.<br>PV of D1–D3 = 3,540 + 3,289 + 3,057 = <b>VND 9,886</b>.</p><p>Terminal value at end of year 3 = D4 ÷ (r − g₂) = 4,772 ÷ (0.13 − 0.03) = 4,772 ÷ 0.10 = <b>VND 47,720</b>.<br>PV of terminal value = 47,720 × 0.6931 = <b>VND 33,075</b>.</p><p>Total value = 9,886 + 33,075 = <b>VND 42,961</b>.</p><p>(c) The two-stage value is about <b>14% lower</b>. The reason is that the perpetual growth rate falls from 5% to 3%, which widens the perpetuity denominator from 0.08 to 0.10 — and since value is inversely proportional to that denominator, a two-percentage-point reduction in long-run growth reduces the capitalised value by roughly a fifth.</p><p>The wider lesson is that the single-stage model's assumption of <b>constant growth in perpetuity</b> is rarely realistic: competitive advantage fades, and a growth rate above the long-run nominal growth of the economy cannot be sustained indefinitely. Multi-stage models make the fade explicit. In both cases the terminal value dominates — here it is 77% of the total — so the long-run growth assumption, not the near-term forecast, is what the valuation actually rests on.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 0.4 · (b) explicit-period PVs and terminal value correctly discounted (0.9) · (c) explanation via the denominator and the fade rationale (0.5) · terminal-value dominance observation (0.2)." },

{ id:"M5-04", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what the residual income model is and when it is preferable to a dividend discount model.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>residual income model</b> values equity as the current <b>book value</b> plus the present value of future <b>residual income</b>, where residual income for a period is:</p><p><b>RI = Net income − (cost of equity × opening book value)</b></p><p>That is, the profit earned <i>in excess of</i> a capital charge for the equity employed. If a company earns exactly its cost of equity, residual income is zero and the shares are worth book value; if it earns more, the shares are worth a premium to book; if less, a discount. This is why it explains the relationship between P/B and ROE so directly.</p><p><b>When it is preferable to the DDM.</b> (1) The company <b>pays no dividends</b>, or its payout bears no relation to earning power — the DDM is then unusable or arbitrary. (2) <b>Cash flows are negative or erratic</b> in the forecast period, as for a growing company, making both DDM and DCF unstable. (3) A large part of value is recognised <b>immediately</b> in book value rather than deferred into a terminal value, so the valuation depends less on the most uncertain assumption — a significant practical advantage, since terminal value often carries 70–80% of a DCF. (4) It suits firms whose assets are <b>fairly stated</b> on the balance sheet, notably financial institutions, which is why it is common in bank valuation.</p><p><b>The limitation</b> is its dependence on <b>accounting quality</b>: book value and earnings must be reliable, and adjustments are needed where accounting policy distorts them.</p>",
  rubric:"Formula and interpretation with the P/B–ROE link (0.8) · at least three circumstances where it is preferable (0.8) · accounting-quality limitation (0.4)." },

{ id:"M5-05", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what a spin-off is, how it differs from a carve-out, and why a company might choose either.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>spin-off</b> distributes the shares of a subsidiary <b>pro rata to the parent's existing shareholders</b>. No cash changes hands and no new investor is introduced: shareholders simply end up holding two separate listed companies instead of one. It is usually structured to be tax-efficient.</p><p>An <b>equity carve-out</b> sells a <b>minority stake</b> in the subsidiary to new investors through an IPO. The parent <b>raises cash</b> and establishes a public market price for the subsidiary while <b>retaining control</b>.</p><p><b>Choosing between them.</b> A <b>spin-off</b> is preferred where the objective is purely to let the market value two dissimilar businesses separately — closing the conglomerate discount — where the parent does not need cash, and where a clean and complete separation is wanted so that each company can pursue its own strategy, capital structure and investor base.</p><p>A <b>carve-out</b> is preferred where the parent <b>needs capital</b>, where it wishes to retain strategic control of the subsidiary, where it wants to establish a valuation benchmark before a possible later full separation, or where the subsidiary needs its own currency to make acquisitions and incentivise management. Carve-outs are frequently a <b>first step</b>, later followed by a spin-off or sale of the residual stake.</p><p>Both are responses to a <b>sum-of-the-parts</b> analysis showing that the group trades below the aggregate value of its divisions.</p>",
  rubric:"Both transactions correctly defined incl. cash and control consequences (0.8) · reasons for choosing a spin-off (0.5) · reasons for choosing a carve-out incl. the staged approach (0.5) · SOTP as the common trigger (0.2)." },

{ id:"M5-06", ch:5, type:"case", clo:"CLO2", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A company wishes to list but its financial statements show large loans to entities owned by the chairman's family. Analyse the problem and advise on the remedial steps.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — the problem.</b> These are <b>related-party transactions</b>, and they are the single issue institutional investors scrutinise most closely in a controlled company.</p><p>The concerns are: (1) <b>Value extraction</b> — company funds have been advanced to entities benefiting the controlling family, possibly on non-commercial terms, at the expense of the company and its future minority shareholders. (2) <b>Recoverability</b> — the loans may be uncollectible, meaning assets and therefore equity are overstated. (3) <b>Governance failure</b> — the transactions indicate the board did not act independently of the controlling shareholder. (4) <b>Disclosure and legal risk</b> — such transactions must be disclosed in the prospectus, and the regulator may query them; inadequate disclosure creates prospectus liability. (5) <b>Valuation impact</b> — investors will apply a <b>governance discount</b>, or refuse to participate, materially reducing the achievable offer price. (6) <b>Precedent</b> — investors will assume the practice continues after listing unless it is demonstrably stopped.</p><p><b>Remedial steps.</b> First, <b>identify and quantify</b> every related-party balance and transaction across the group, with independent verification. Second, <b>repay or unwind</b> the loans before listing — ideally settled in cash by the related parties, which is the only fully satisfactory outcome; where repayment is impossible, the balances must be provided against, with the effect on earnings and equity reflected in the accounts and in the valuation. Third, for any relationship that must continue for genuine commercial reasons, <b>formalise it on documented arm's-length terms</b> with independent valuation support. Fourth, <b>strengthen governance</b> so recurrence is prevented: appoint independent directors, establish an audit committee with authority over related-party approvals, adopt a written related-party policy requiring independent approval and abstention by conflicted parties, and implement internal audit. Fifth, <b>disclose fully</b> in the prospectus, including the historic position and the remediation. Sixth, obtain <b>auditor comfort</b> on the restated position.</p><p><b>Advice to the client.</b> This is not merely a compliance exercise. Investors price this risk directly, so cleaning it up before listing will be recovered in the offer price — the argument that usually persuades a reluctant controlling family.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four problems incl. value extraction, recoverability and governance (1.2) · at least four remedial steps incl. unwinding, arm's-length formalisation and governance reform (1.2) · disclosure and auditor comfort (0.3) · commercial framing of the advice (0.3)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 6 ================== */
{ id:"M6-01", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what liquidity means in a securities market and the indicators used to measure it.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Liquidity</b> is the ability to trade a meaningful quantity of a security <b>quickly</b>, at a price <b>close to the prevailing market price</b>, and at <b>low transaction cost</b>. A liquid market allows an investor to convert a position into cash without materially moving the price against themselves.</p><p><b>Indicators.</b></p><ul><li><b>Bid-ask spread</b> — the most direct measure. Narrow spreads mean low cost of immediacy; spreads widen when dealers face higher inventory and adverse-selection risk.</li><li><b>Depth</b> — the volume available at and near the best bid and offer, showing how large an order the book can absorb without moving the price.</li><li><b>Trading volume and turnover ratio</b> (volume relative to free float), indicating how actively the security changes hands.</li><li><b>Price impact / slippage</b> — how far the execution price of a given order size deviates from the pre-trade price; the most direct measure for a large investor.</li><li><b>Resilience</b> — how quickly the book replenishes and the price recovers after a large trade.</li><li><b>Free float</b> — a small float structurally limits liquidity however active trading appears.</li></ul><p><b>Why it matters.</b> Illiquid securities trade at a <b>discount</b>, because investors demand compensation for the difficulty of exit. Liquidity is also <b>state-dependent</b>: it evaporates precisely in stressed markets, when spreads widen, depth collapses and investors most need to sell — which is why liquidity risk must be managed in advance rather than assumed.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition covering speed, price and cost (0.6) · at least four indicators correctly explained (0.9) · liquidity discount and state-dependence (0.5)." },

{ id:"M6-02", ch:6, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"An investor buys 8,000 shares at VND 65,000 using 50% margin. Maintenance margin is 30%. (a) Compute equity, loan and the margin-call price. (b) At VND 45,000, is a call triggered, and what is the return on equity?",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Position value = 8,000 × 65,000 = <b>VND 520 million</b>.<br>Equity = 50% × 520m = <b>VND 260 million</b>; loan = <b>VND 260 million</b>.<br>Margin call price = 65,000 × (1 − 0.50) ÷ (1 − 0.30) = 65,000 × 0.5 ÷ 0.7 = <b>VND 46,429</b>.</p><p>(b) At VND 45,000: market value = 8,000 × 45,000 = <b>VND 360 million</b>.<br>Equity = 360 − 260 = <b>VND 100 million</b>.<br>Equity ratio = 100 ÷ 360 = <b>27.8%</b>, which is below the 30% maintenance requirement — and VND 45,000 is below the call price of VND 46,429. <b>A margin call is triggered.</b></p><p>Return on equity = (100 − 260) ÷ 260 = <b>−61.5%</b> (before interest costs).</p><p>By comparison the unlevered return would be (45,000 − 65,000) ÷ 65,000 = <b>−30.8%</b> — the 2× leverage has exactly doubled the loss. The investor must now deposit funds or securities to restore the 30% ratio, failing which the broker will liquidate part or all of the position, crystallising the loss and removing any possibility of benefiting from a subsequent recovery.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) equity, loan and call price (0.8) · (b) equity, ratio and correct call conclusion (0.6) · levered return with the unlevered comparison (0.6)." },

{ id:"M6-03", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the risks a securities firm faces in proprietary trading and the controls that manage them.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — risks.</b> <b>Market risk</b> — losses from adverse price, rate or volatility movements on positions held. <b>Liquidity risk</b> — inability to exit a position at a reasonable price, especially in a concentrated or illiquid holding. <b>Concentration risk</b> from excessive exposure to a single security, sector or counterparty. <b>Model risk</b>, where valuations or hedges depend on assumptions that fail. <b>Operational risk</b> — errors, system failure and unauthorised or concealed trading. <b>Regulatory and conduct risk</b> — breaching position limits, trading on inside information, market manipulation, or failing to keep proprietary activity properly separated from client business. <b>Reputational risk</b> if the firm is seen to trade against its own clients.</p><p><b>Controls.</b> A board-approved <b>risk appetite</b> translated into <b>position, concentration and VaR limits</b>, monitored in real time with automatic escalation of breaches. <b>Stress testing</b> against extreme scenarios, recognising that VaR says nothing about the tail. <b>Stop-loss</b> discipline and defined exit rules. <b>Segregation of duties</b> — traders may not value, confirm or settle their own trades — supported by <b>independent price verification</b> and <b>daily P&amp;L attribution</b>, so unexplained profit is investigated. <b>Client order priority</b> with separate accounts, systems and personnel from brokerage. <b>Mandatory block leave</b> so another person must run the book, a classic method of surfacing concealment. <b>Surveillance</b> of trading around research publication and client orders. And <b>deferred, adjustable remuneration</b> so that profit later shown to be illusory can be clawed back.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four risks correctly identified (0.8) · at least five controls (0.9) · segregation of duties and independent verification specifically (0.3)." },

{ id:"M6-04", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain how electronic trading has changed the brokerage business and how firms have responded.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — changes.</b> <b>Commission compression.</b> Electronic execution removed most of the cost and skill from order handling, and price competition — culminating in zero-commission offerings in several markets — has driven brokerage commission toward zero as a revenue line. <b>Disintermediation of the adviser</b>: clients place their own orders through platforms and access information directly, so the broker's traditional informational advantage has largely disappeared. <b>Speed and scale</b> have become decisive, with volumes fragmenting across venues and algorithmic execution replacing manual working of orders. <b>Retail participation</b> has expanded dramatically, bringing large numbers of less experienced investors into the market. And <b>technology has become the principal fixed cost</b>, favouring scale and driving consolidation.</p><p><b>Responses.</b> Firms have shifted revenue toward <b>financing</b> — margin lending and securities lending — which is now the principal monetisation of the retail relationship. They have added <b>value-added services</b>: research, advisory, wealth management, structured products and derivatives. Some accept <b>payment for order flow</b>, funding zero commissions but creating a best-execution conflict that regulators scrutinise or restrict. Others compete on <b>platform quality</b> — speed, tools, data and user experience — or pursue <b>scale</b> through consolidation, or specialise in segments where advice still commands a fee.</p><p>The strategic consequence is that a broker is now substantially a <b>technology and financing</b> business rather than an execution intermediary — which also means its risks have shifted toward credit, operational and cyber risk.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four changes incl. commission compression and technology cost (0.8) · at least four responses incl. financing and value-added services (0.8) · strategic conclusion or the PFOF conflict (0.4)." },

{ id:"M6-05", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the arguments for and against permitting short selling in a developing market such as Vietnam.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — arguments in favour.</b> <b>Price discovery</b>: without short selling only optimists can express a view, so prices embed only positive opinions and overvaluation persists. Short sellers incorporate negative information, making prices more accurate. <b>Liquidity</b>: shorts add two-way flow and are essential to market making, since a dealer must be able to sell stock it does not own to quote continuously. <b>Fraud detection</b>: short sellers have a financial incentive to investigate and expose accounting fraud and overstatement, and have historically done so ahead of regulators. <b>Hedging and derivatives</b>: short selling underpins arbitrage, index and derivatives markets, without which those products cannot be priced or hedged efficiently.</p><p><b>Arguments against.</b> <b>Amplification</b>: in a falling market short selling adds supply and can accelerate declines, particularly where margin calls are simultaneously forcing sales. <b>Settlement risk</b> from naked short selling, where the seller has not located a borrow and may fail to deliver — a serious concern where the lending market is underdeveloped. <b>Manipulation</b> risk, through rumour combined with short positions, in a market with a large, less experienced retail base. <b>Infrastructure requirements</b>: short selling needs a functioning securities-lending market, reliable custody, effective surveillance and settlement discipline, which take time to build. <b>Investor protection and confidence</b> concerns in a market where financial literacy is still developing.</p><p><b>Balanced conclusion.</b> The mainstream position is that short selling should be permitted but <b>covered</b> — requiring a located borrow — and subject to disclosure of large positions, an eligible-securities list, and the capacity for temporary restrictions in stressed conditions. Vietnam's cautious approach reflects genuine infrastructure and protection concerns rather than an objection in principle, and liberalisation appropriately follows the development of the securities-lending and settlement framework.</p>",
  rubric:"At least three arguments in favour (0.7) · at least three against (0.7) · balanced conclusion identifying covered short selling with safeguards (0.6)." },

{ id:"M6-06", ch:6, type:"case", clo:"CLO3", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A securities firm's margin book has grown rapidly and is concentrated in a few mid-cap stocks with thin free floats. Analyse the risks and recommend action.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — risks.</b> (1) <b>Correlated collateral deterioration.</b> Because many clients have borrowed against the same few stocks, a fall in those names hits the entire book simultaneously; the diversification the lender implicitly assumed does not exist. (2) <b>Liquidation infeasibility.</b> With thin free floats, the firm's collateral may represent a large multiple of daily traded volume. In a force-sale it cannot realise the position at screen prices — its own selling moves the market against it, so realised recovery is far below the marked value. (3) <b>Reflexivity.</b> Margin lending may itself have inflated these stocks: leveraged buying pushed prices up, higher prices supported larger loans, which financed further buying. Such a loop reverses violently. (4) <b>Price-band trapping.</b> If the stocks lock limit-down, the firm cannot sell at any price for successive sessions while losses accumulate. (5) <b>Client and legal risk</b> from force-sales and disputed calls, plus reputational damage. (6) <b>Capital and funding risk</b>, since the firm funds these loans and may face its own liquidity pressure.</p><p><b>Recommended action.</b> <b>Immediately</b>: quantify exposure per security as a multiple of average daily volume and of free float — the critical metric, not the loan's nominal size; stress-test recovery assuming realistic liquidation over several days at declining prices; and stop further lending against the concentrated names.</p><p><b>Structurally</b>: impose <b>hard concentration limits</b> per security, per client and per group of related clients, and a limit expressed relative to free float and daily volume; review the <b>eligible securities list</b>, removing or applying materially lower loan-to-value ratios to thin, volatile names; require additional margin for concentrated positions; and reduce existing exposure in an orderly, pre-emptive way rather than waiting for a forced sale.</p><p><b>Ongoing</b>: intra-day mark-to-market with early client alerts before the maintenance level is breached; monitoring of <b>market-wide</b> margin balances, since a system-wide deleveraging will hit the firm's collateral regardless of its own prudence; and clear board reporting of concentration metrics. The governing principle is that in margin lending the binding constraint is not the value of the collateral but the firm's <b>ability to sell it</b>.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four risks incl. correlated deterioration and liquidation infeasibility (1.2) · reflexivity or price-band trapping (0.3) · immediate actions incl. exposure relative to volume/float and stress testing (0.7) · structural limits and eligible-list revision (0.6) · closing principle (0.2)." }

];
